ity and if you thrive when given a blank slate to write your own playbook, this role is for you.</p><h2><strong>Success Metrics (First 90 Days)</strong></h2><ul><li><p><strong>30 Days: </strong>Take full ownership of the existing marketing tech stack and establish baseline performance metrics.</p></li><li><p><strong>60 Days: </strong>Fully automate the core content generation pipeline and establish QA workflows.</p></li><li><p><strong>90 Days: </strong>Launch at least two multi-channel growth experiments and demonstrate a measurable reduction in CAC or an increase in content throughput.</p></li></ul><p></p><h2><strong>Core Responsibilities</strong></h2><h3><strong>1. Storytelling That Converts</strong></h3><ul><li><p><strong>Making Complexity Clear:</strong> Take complex, often highly technical capabilities and features and turn them into clear, digestible stories without losing accuracy.</p></li><li><p><strong>Audience-First Messaging:</strong> Understand who the campaigns speak to (developers, technical decision-makers, business stakeholders or end users) and frame each capability around what that audience cares about, at the right level of technical depth.</p></li><li><p><strong>Spark Curiosity & Action:</strong> Craft narratives that get audiences interested and excited, and that move them to the desired conversion.</p></li><li><p><strong>Product & Engineering Collaboration:</strong> Work closely with product and engineering teams to deeply understand what is being built, so every story is both compelling and technically correct.</p></li></ul><h3><strong>2. Tactical Deployment & Agile Execution</strong></h3><ul><li><p><strong>Multi-Channel Campaign Launch:</strong> Execute and launch integrated campaigns across communities, social channels, search, newsletters, loyalty programs, influencers, paid ads and landing pages. Where needed, bring in and coordinate additional third parties to support execution, such as external PR firms and campaign support providers.</p